<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khumarawayh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">864–<a href="/years/896">896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Khumarawayh ibn Ahmad ibn Tulun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khumarawayh was the second Tulunid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a> in 884. After fighting the Abbasid regent al-Muwaffaq, he reached a treaty in 886 that recognised his rule over Egypt and Syria in return for tribute. He married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatr-al-nada">Qatr al-Nada</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> with a famously lavish dowry, but his extravagance emptied the treasury, and he was murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khumarawayh.jpg" alt="Khumarawayh" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, 9th-century mint masters · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Khumarawayh_ibn_Ahmad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
